body:
The strict "real I/O" boundary that separates unit/integration from e2e (the crux of the law), exact and without exception — "real I/O" means ANY of:
- ANY filesystem access, INCLUDING temp directories (creating, reading, writing, or deleting a file or directory anywhere; `tempfile`/`TempDir` included);
- ANY database access, INCLUDING opening a local `db.lbug` (any lbug open, read-only or read-write);
- ANY git operation (any git2 call or git CLI invocation, including `git init`, `rev-parse`, commits, worktree ops);
- ANY process spawn (including spawning the apg binary itself).

Therefore a unit or integration test must be PURE IN-MEMORY, with fakes for filesystem, database, git, and process. Any test that touches a temp dir, writes node files, opens `db.lbug`, runs git, or spawns the apg binary is e2e BY DEFINITION, however it is named or located.

AC (review-checked prose law, never executed): given a test's body, its tier is decidable by the boundary above; a test performing any listed action is not counted as unit/integration; the split of the existing suite assigns every real-I/O test to e2e.
